1480: These same charts prove proper discontinuity and the
1481: Hausdorff property. On a full-rank face, a nonzero
1482: period cannot stabilize a bounded polyhedral cell,
1483: because it translates that cell by the nonzero
1484: vector \(P_0n\) or \(Hn\). Only finitely many
1485: translates of a compact chart meet it. On a rank-one
1486: face its possible stabilizer is the explicitly
1487: identified kernel; its strict norm contraction
1488: meets a compact shell only for finitely many
1489: iterates. The estimates are uniform on the
1490: neighbouring charts. Thus the full equivalence
1491: relation is locally a finite union of closed
1492: graphs on these compact covers. Its quotient is
1493: Hausdorff, and inverse images of compact smaller
1494: base sets are compact.
1495: 
1496: Over \(v\ne0\) the chart comparison is the specified
1497: family, not only a birational torus comparison.
1498: On the selected divisor the I-face is its chosen
1499: fan. On the other nodal divisor the W-face is the
1500: actual source Tate chain and degree-zero line
1501: with gluing \(c=(QP)^a\), followed by the actual
1502: kernel contraction (SZ.45). This is the ordinary
1503: nonzero-line source filling under the explicit
1504: theta/Cartier map of Section 19. The E face and
1505: every H modification lie over \(v=0\).
1506: The finite-base pieces glue by the two literal
1507: deck squares (SZ.50)--(SZ.51). All their physical
1508: delta characters agree on the common nonzero
1509: line charts.
1510: 
1511: This assembles the normal analytic period quotient
1512: and its finite-base partial attachment. A
1513: period-equivariant toric resolution can subsequently
1514: be performed relative to the already smooth I,
1515: W and primitive E boundary subfans. Its new ray
1516: multiplicities must be retained. The resulting
1517: oriented boundary, the fixed-stratum incidence
1518: and the characteristic pairing have not been
1519: computed in this subsection; none is replaced
1520: by the determinant or fundamental-region count.
1521: 
1522: The base multiplicities are also literal. Since
1523: \(q_1-q_2=T_1(v)-T_0(v)=v\,u_v(v)\), with
1524: \(u_v(0)=4\), the I corner has
1525: \(q_1=xy,q_2=y\) and
1526: \(v=y(x-1)/u_v(v)\); the W corner has
1527: \(q_2=xy,q_1=y\) and
1528: \(v=y(1-x)/u_v(v)\). The E corner uses
1529: \(v=xy,q_1=y\). Thus an integral ray with
1530: base coordinates \((r,s)\) has \(v\)-order
1531: \(s,s,r+s\) in the I, W, E sectors respectively.
1532: These orders apply to the primitive integral
1533: rays of the actual refinement. They retain
1534: every higher H multiplicity. Each normal
1535: toric chart is the closure of its dense
1536: torus over the nonzero parameter, so no
1537: total-space component is contained in the
1538: central fibre, and its
1539: parameter function is a nonzero divisor.
1540: Its local rings are therefore torsion free
1541: over the one-dimensional parameter ring,
1542: which proves flatness over the \(v\)-disc.
1543: 
1544: ## 23. Remaining original-u cap-root endpoint dictionary
